# The Sacrifice
Tamil title: பலி

“How did you dare drive me from the palace of your heart, my king? What fault have you found in me? Are these the very eyes from which the light of love once streamed, now pouring forth such cruelty? When I accepted you as my husband, you could scarcely have imagined that a place in the royal court would one day be yours. You were merely one among the commanders serving the country when I took you as my beloved. Now that sovereignty has come to you, must I be banished to the wilderness?

“Was I not the woman whose heart overflowed at the sight of your ascent? My lord’s valour and resolution are becoming a mighty epic. France itself lies at my darling’s feet. In the military tempest that moves wherever his pointing finger directs it, empires surrender. The world praises the man who has made a temple of my heart. The whole earth hails him as a man whom none can subdue, a man of immense power and a fearless heart. Yet that guardian remains bound by my love!

“When people mocked me, saying, ‘Was a soldier the only man she could find? In which battlefield will he lose a leg and become a cripple, or lose his eyes and become blind? She has married him; was her beauty so deficient?’ I would think, ‘Fools, have you now seen what manner of man my husband is? Crowns lie at his feet. Kings are his attendants. Provinces are playthings to him!’ O man of majestic stride, who has planted the flag of victory in all eight directions! Delight of my heart, light of my life, beloved who gave me a new world: have you dared cast me aside? Me? Your companion in pleasure? Is it truly so? Am I seeing a dreadful dream, or has some ruinous thought really driven you to torment me?

“My lord, you have hacked enemies to pieces, fed them to eagles and foxes, and danced in delight crying, ‘Victory! Victory!’ That is a warrior’s pastime. Has torturing me also become a heroic sport fit for you? I would not grieve if you cut me to pieces. Instead you let me live and have set out to pierce my heart with a thousand spears. The man whom I saw with joyful pride and claimed as my husband: must another woman now claim him as hers? Must I hear of it while lying abandoned in some corner? Must another beauty embrace the body I embraced, while shame and anguish pull me apart and leave me writhing?

“Will you wander through palace and flower garden with a new woman in the moonlight, while I, rejected and driven away, sob in widow’s garb though my husband still lives? Will women melt at the sight of me and say, ‘Look, poor Josephine! Rejected by her husband, she suffers without refuge,’ while children clap their hands and laugh?

“Beloved, before you carried me to your realm of pleasure, do you not know how many young men yearned for the nectar of my lips? If they should now see me driven from the palace, what taunts will they use to kill me? I have heard people say that nations tremble merely at the sound of the name Napoleon. Now the very thought of that name makes me tremble! Why take the pearl from its shell, let it gladden the mind for a while, and then smash it into a hundred pieces and fling it into the mud? I was a pearl within my shell. You smiled and spoke; I resisted. You sought my love; I told myself it was too late in life. You conquered my heart. The feast I gave you, my lord, was surely not without flavour!

“You call it fruitless because there is no child. I am ashamed, but I imagined that our life of love belonged wholly to us and that perhaps the Lord would not allow even a child to intrude upon it. Why has your mind begun to think otherwise? My king, does the failure of our love to yield ‘fruit’ make it bitter to you? Let me remind you of one thing. I am a sinner unable to give you the nectar of a child, but I am not barren; I have two sons.

“Learned people say that a rock will not melt however often one speaks to it. Man of virtue, who called me ‘sweet-tongued one’ in endearment and sported with me as though asking whether you were a conqueror only on the battlefield and not in love as well: do not kill me. Do not leave me helpless. I cannot endure it. No person with a heart could dare strip love of its splendour. When sovereignty came to you, did you not bring me here as the woman who dwelt in your heart? Must she now be sacrificed to the demon of office?

“No, sovereign, no! Do not cast me into consuming fire and then anoint your chest with fragrant sandalwood. Do not turn my tears into rosewater. Do not scorch and destroy my love. When you walk with royal majesty through the audience hall beside a fresh young bride, you cannot help thinking that this unfortunate woman, who lived with you, lived for you, and loved you with all her heart, has lost her radiance and honour and is staggering towards the pit of death. You certainly cannot avoid that thought. My lord, it will not leave you in peace. Your crown will become a crown of thorns; your throne, a seat of serpents; your ceremonial hall, a chamber of cruelty; your heart, a hundred shattered pieces.

“Yes, I swear upon pure love: after betraying me, you cannot find a new fragrance and live happily. We were created for each other. Must I remind you that any woman who agrees to marry you now will not come because she loves you? Napoleon is guardian of an empire. By marrying him, a woman gains position, authority, wealth, and influence. That alone will bring some princess to you. I rejoiced at seeing you, not so the woman who comes now. She will rejoice at the ornaments in the palace, at servants standing with folded hands, and at the ceremonial dolls waiting in the royal court. It will assuredly not be you she delights in. I found joy in your heart; she comes for the possessions amassed around you.

“As your station rises, my lord, must the love in your heart dry up? What manner of justice is this? Was I fit to be your companion only while you were an ordinary soldier fighting bitter battles? Once you sit enthroned as king, must another maiden take my place? We wear one garment in the rains and another in spring. Does noble love lodged in the heart also change with the season? I cannot even comprehend it. Who instructed you? From whom did you learn such treachery? What lover stirred up this rebellion?

“Mighty ruler, now that greatness has come to you, affection and devotion will well up in many people. Can you believe them true? We see birds circling a ripened field, bats around a fruiting tree, herons waiting hungrily, and spiders weaving their nets even among humankind. Someone in that company has changed your mind. Do not do this, warrior elevated by victory; do not roast my heart.

“Perhaps you think, ‘What can this poor woman do?’ When no one has the power to oppose you even in battle, perhaps you think she knows nothing except to shed a torrent of tears. My darling, these tears are unlike any weapon you have yet encountered. They have the power to burn to ashes the deceit that builds a fortress of stone around the heart and hides within it. Do not mock me as one who raves. Do not be surprised if I lose my reason, wander the broad streets of Paris recalling former days and singing songs of love, embrace lampposts, and dance in disarray with loosened clothes and fallen hair. What happens to a little child rolled down from a mountaintop? My mind is being brought to just that state.”

All this and more rose within her. A thousand thoughts boiled in her heart, but Josephine could not utter a single word. She gave one cry and fainted. She fell and lay without breath or speech. Napoleon too was shaken.

Josephine had borne no child to him. The French Empire had no prince.

Napoleon was emperor. He had won victory and fame for the nation. The Revolution had been crushed; the new order had been cut down. This lion of the battlefield, who began his journey as a soldier, had become an emperor. Yet according to hereditary custom there remained one more achievement an emperor must secure. Napoleon had not secured it. There was no child, no heir.

What would become of the empire won by expending all his strength and treating dangers as straw if Napoleon had no heir? This poisonous thought entered his heart, grew rapidly, and brought him to a disastrous decision: he would put Josephine away and marry a princess. She would become a mother and give France a prince; the dynasty would endure. He resolved to carry it through. Who could stop Napoleon once he had made a decision?

Napoleon had always pursued the radiance of fame. The great demon of ambition, the lust for office, burned in his heart. If even love obstructed the success of his design, must it not be crushed and thrown aside? That was Napoleon’s way. How could the beauty who became his companion and, when he was made emperor, shone as empress, have known it? She believed her husband might do anything and turn his heart to iron for any other purpose, but towards her would always remain the beloved who poured out love.

Poor fool! An empire now seemed greater to him. Could he still delight in the poetry your eyes had given him? He was Emperor Napoleon. Napoleon once begged you for the gift of love, enchantress; the emperor now asked for a princess, for he wanted a prince. Could a crowned monarch remain without an heir? A king’s son must become king. Would he destroy that ‘sacred tradition’? You lie remembering how he covered your eyes and held your hand. Those were the games of love, the lore of romance. He now acted according to ‘royal policy.’ Who could tell Josephine this? All the beauties around her stood stunned. None had imagined Napoleon would make so terrible a decision.

Napoleon told Josephine, “For the welfare of the empire, my enchantress, I have decided to put you away and marry a woman of another royal house. France needs a prince. It is my duty to provide him.”

This was the same Napoleon who once stood petitioning Josephine for the ‘domestic flame’ that would illuminate his home. She had not met and bewitched him in a market, nor had he glimpsed her in a flower garden and let her enter his thoughts. He had learned where she lived, gone there, paid his respects, knelt and begged her not to refuse him, and married her. Even the circumstances that enabled him to win Josephine were an epic capable of delighting poets.

Now she lay faint at the betrayer’s feet. In another palace, surrounded by attendants, a princess was adorning herself to become wife to this cruel man and empress of France.

The woman lying below, without breath or speech, had offered her heart to this hard-hearted man who fought fierce battles across the field. Another woman was coming: a decorative doll raised in a palace, coming not to give love but to produce an ‘heir.’ Great French officials skilled in statecraft would go to ask for her hand and bring that swan back. Here lay a dovecote bird with broken wings. Napoleon had once said everything imaginable to win this speaking dove.

At first Napoleon and Josephine did not even know one another. People had expected the flower of revolution to spread the fragrance of good government. Then it was called a poisonous flower, and military rule entered to compel the people to obey strict discipline. Napoleon had been appointed to suppress the agitators of Paris.

One day a young man, still of school age, came to his office, saluted him, and humbly submitted a petition. “My father, honoured officer, was killed by the revolutionaries. Ours is a respectable family that took no interest in political disturbances. All I ask of you, whose great power has restored peace to the country, is that my father’s sword be returned to us. His heroic blade is an emblem of our family’s honour. As his son, I have undertaken to recover that sword. I beg you graciously to order its return.”

Napoleon gave him the sword. The boy received it on his knees, clasped it to his breast, touched it reverently to his eyes, and gazed at it with a melting heart as tears of joy flowed. Napoleon marvelled that a son should venerate his father’s sword so deeply. What attachment to family honour, what pride! It must be a family of the highest quality, he thought, and the mother who had raised him to such nobility deserved reverence.

He saw her and knew she was worthy of love.

Josephine was a widow living simply but without depending upon others, with two sons whom she cherished like the pupils of her eyes. Widow’s dress had not destroyed her allure. Her shapely beauty remained, though she lived without casting nets with her eyes. Napoleon saw that lovely flower and surrendered his heart.

He had gone there expecting to see an old woman rolling her rosary in the garb of age, but found a beautiful peahen. He went to compliment her by saying, “Your son is noble; the country owes a debt to mothers who produce men like him.” Instead he saw a peahen that had lost none of its splendour though it did not spread its plumage and dance. The visitor who came to pay a compliment found himself speaking of love.

She did not accept him at once. She was two years older than he and the mother of two sons. She could only remain quiet and wonder whether to accept the soldier’s love or dismiss it as one of the many waves that rise and vanish. At last she gave herself to Napoleon.

Even when exhausted after a day of fierce battle and about to sleep, Napoleon would think of Josephine and compose love letters. Such was his passion. Now he had cut her down and planned to take another fine-featured woman as his empress.

Historians offer many explanations for the cruelties Napoleon committed to magnify his glory. Of all his cruelties, none can equal his betrayal of Josephine.

The day on which Josephine must leave the palace was fixed; the order had been issued. Napoleon, emperor and host to the demon of ambition, was not a man restrained by tears.

That queenly woman covered herself completely in a long robe. Palace maids stood weeping on the staircase. They said nothing; they were in no condition to speak. She waved farewell, left the palace, and entered the carriage waiting for her, drawn by six horses. She travelled away carrying the cruelty of the heartless man to whom she had given her heart. Who can recall that day without a melting heart?

Josephine’s heart raged, but what else could she do? Even those who opposed Napoleon could scarcely survive. What could this poor, vulnerable woman do? She had the right to speak words of fire and condemn his scheming mind, but she had surrendered her heart and so bore the cruelty. Perhaps she thought, “Let us endure it; let time teach its lesson.”

A new empress entered the ceremonial hall: Marie Louise, princess of Austria. She entered only the ceremonial hall, not the conqueror’s heart. She delighted in making Napoleon’s fame a jewelled lamp for her palace; the light of love did not blossom in her eyes. Her duty was merely to be empress, and she performed it while opportunity remained.

Napoleon’s fortunes began to decline. Marie found the courage to leave him. Only if she had given him a place in her heart would removing him have been impossible; Marie suffered no such trouble or agitation.

Napoleon’s fall took shape faster than his rise. Everything he touched turned bad. Enmity flared in every direction. The army that had known nothing but victory lost its footing. The cruel cold of Russia descended upon it. Prophets said his sovereignty would collapse. Hoping for one more resolute attack and one magnificent victory that would restore the light of fame, he was disappointed again and again and lay with an anguished heart.

Josephine had been living like a wildflower with a scorched heart. Learning the fate that had befallen her beloved husband, she melted and wept. He too recalled royal luxury, the arrogance it produced, the wrong that drove him to seek a new wife, and the cruelty of expelling his beloved for that wrongful design. The man who had never known distress was distressed. He sent Josephine a letter describing his condition.

Josephine suffered even more than she had while descending the palace stairs that day. “Alas, has this fate befallen him? Has this condition come to the man who once paraded while the world praised him?” she thought in sorrow.

She possessed the nobility to write in this spirit: “I would fly to you, remain at your side, serve you, and share your grief, my lord. I can do it; my heart allows it. But what would the world say? Another woman has the right to you. For me, the woman cast aside, to come to your dwelling would not be proper. That thought alone torments me.” Despite all the cruelties he had inflicted, she had not forgotten her love for him.

Napoleon had become a tiger caught in a trap. The trap was strong, and the tiger’s teeth and claws had lost their lustre.

Napoleon was at Elba, stripped of strength and splendour and surrounded by darkness. Josephine lay ill. Perhaps she thought of him all day and dreamed of former times all night. The flowering branch withered; it became clear it would die.

There at Elba, Napoleon was in such a condition that many could say, “How can he ever rise again? His dominion has been extinguished.” Four weeks after Napoleon went to Elba, disease consumed Josephine. On a lovely evening she died.

As life departed, Napoleon remained in her heart. In her hand was a medallion bearing Napoleon’s likeness. Upon her lips were the words, “Napoleon! Elba... Napoleon!”

(Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 29 January 1956.)

---NOTES---
The title “பலி” means “sacrifice” or “victim offered in sacrifice”; the article presents Josephine as the sacrifice made to Napoleon’s dynastic ambition.
“சரசாங்கி” is an ornate term of address for an enchanting or amorous woman; “my enchantress” conveys its function here.
“அடைப்பக்காரர்கள்” is an old courtly term for attendants or ceremonial retainers.
The scan prints Marie Louise’s name as “மேரி லூசியா”; the translation uses the historically established English name while retaining the printed Tamil unchanged in the source section.
